        <h2>Summary / Description</h2>
        <div class="description">
          OBJECTIVES:<br>
Bioprocessing of cell cultures in space requires knowledge of cell behavior in microgravity. The attachment of cells to growth surfaces on Earth is normally affected by settling. The feasibility of cell-to-bead attachment in ambient temperature was shown in DSO 413. The Incubator Cell Attachment Test (ICAT) was used to test the behavior of cell attachment in weightlessness in a thirty-seven degrees Celsius incubator.
<p>
APPROACH:<br>
The incubator contained 4 cell culture chambers of human embryonic kidney (HEK) cells in a growth medium, 4 syringes loaded with Cytodex 3 microcarrier beads, and 4 syringes containing a glutaraldehyde fixative. The apparatus was installed in the Shuttle flight deck 14 hours before launch and kept at ambient temperature. Four hours after launch the incubator was turned on, and after 3.5 hours the beads were injected into the cell chambers. Samples were fixed by glutaraldehyde injection at timed intervals. After landing, the suspension was evaluated by electron microscopy for cell-to-bead attachment, cell-to-cell aggregation and individual floating cell counts.
<p>
RESULTS:<br>
The greatest percentage of cell attachment occurred within 3 hours of when cells and beads were mixed in microgravity. After 2.5 hours, the number of cell attachments was fifty percent higher in flight samples at thirty-seven degrees Celsius than in the ground control. All of the surface area of a bead is available at low-g, while on Earth only the top hemisphere of a settled bead is available. No differences in the manner of cell attachment, or cell spreading and proliferation on the beads, were observed by electron microscopy.<p>
        </div>

